(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-neuro-ophthalmology-quiz"]={"title":"Neuro-Ophthalmology Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which structure gives the extra one to two millimetres of lid elevation when a patient is startled?","choices":["Ciliary muscle","Muller's muscle","Levator palpebrae superioris","Superior rectus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is sympathetically innervated, which is why Horner syndrome causes mild ptosis. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"How long after dimming the lights is the anisocoria of Horner syndrome most evident?","choices":["Only after pharmacologic testing","It is constant regardless of lighting","Within the first few seconds","After a full minute"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, when a normal pupil would dilate rapidly and actively. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Why are both pupils normally the same size even when one eye is covered?","choices":["The iris muscles are mechanically linked","Sympathetic tone is identical bilaterally","The efferent limb is bilateral","Both retinas share one optic nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Each pretectal nucleus sends impulses to both Edinger-Westphal nuclei. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has an efferent pupillary problem. Why does that make his pupils unequal?","choices":["The efferent command is normally bilateral and equal","Afferent input differs between eyes","The pupils are never truly equal","The iris muscles fatigue independently"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, so inequality means an efferent pathway has failed. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which anatomical relationship explains why an apical lung tumour can cause Horner syndrome?","choices":["The vagus carries sympathetic fibres","Second-order fibres loop over the lung apex","First-order fibres run through the lung","Third-order fibres cross the pleura"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, passing under the subclavian artery. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has ptosis and miosis after COVID-19 requiring intubation. Which association does the lecture note?","choices":["Papilledema after ventilation","Adie pupil after infection","Argyll Robertson pupil after COVID","Horner syndrome after mechanical ventilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with possible lung injury proposed. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What does the lecture say is anatomically unique about the trochlear nerve?","choices":["It supplies two extraocular muscles","It exits through the optic canal","It arises dorsally and crosses","It carries parasympathetic fibres"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, so the left nucleus supplies the right eye. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Where do the first-order sympathetic neurons synapse on their way to the pupil?","choices":["Pretectal nucleus","Edinger-Westphal nucleus","Ciliospinal centre of Budge","Superior cervical ganglion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, at C8 to T2. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man reports the room spinning alongside his nystagmus. Which symptom is this, and what is it commonly associated with?","choices":["Vertigo, with vestibular problems","Oscillopsia, with vestibular problems","Diplopia, with cranial nerve palsy","Amaurosis, with carotid disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is often the primary symptom. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has a third nerve palsy. Which muscles does the inferior division supply?","choices":["Levator palpebrae and superior rectus","Superior oblique alone","Orbicularis oculi","Inferior rectus, medial rectus, inferior oblique"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, along with the parasympathetic fibres. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has ptosis. Which muscle provides the main lid elevation?","choices":["Muller's muscle","Orbicularis oculi","Superior rectus","Levator palpebrae superioris"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, supplied by the oculomotor nerve. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man focuses on a near object. Which globe movement occurs?","choices":["Both eyes adduct","Both eyes abduct","Both eyes elevate","Both eyes depress"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the convergence reflex. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A lesion of the right optic nerve produces which field defect?","choices":["Left homonymous hemianopsia","Superior quadrantanopia","Total blindness of the right eye","Bitemporal hemianopsia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a pre-chiasmal lesion is monocular. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Where is the lesion in an Adie tonic pupil?","choices":["Ciliary ganglion","Edinger-Westphal nucleus","Pretectal nucleus","Superior cervical ganglion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with aberrant reinnervation afterwards. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman developed nystagmus at age three and has no symptoms. What does the lecture say about symptoms in early-onset nystagmus?","choices":["It is usually asymptomatic if acquired before age eight","It is always symptomatic regardless of onset","It becomes symptomatic only in adulthood","It causes diplopia in all cases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why onset age matters. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Where does the lecture suspect the lesion lies in Argyll Robertson pupil?","choices":["Lung apex","Dorsal midbrain","Ciliary ganglion","Optic chiasm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, in the pretectal area of the superior colliculus. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which feature distinguishes ptosis from a third nerve palsy from ptosis of Horner syndrome?","choices":["Patient age","Levator function","Laterality","Time of day"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is reduced in third nerve palsy and normal in Horner syndrome. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Parasympathetic preganglionic fibres leave the Edinger-Westphal nucleus and travel along which nerve?","choices":["Cranial nerve four","Cranial nerve seven","Cranial nerve three","Cranial nerve two"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, synapsing in the ipsilateral ciliary ganglion within the orbit. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What defines pupillary light-near dissociation?","choices":["Dilation on light stimulation","No reaction to light, preserved reaction to near","No reaction to near, preserved reaction to light","Unequal pupils in darkness only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has opioid toxicity. Which two features does the lecture say actually matter clinically?","choices":["Reduced consciousness and respiratory depression","Tachycardia and hypertension","Mydriasis and agitation","Fasciculations and weakness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct; the miosis is a clue rather than the danger. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How many neurons make up the oculo-sympathetic pathway to the pupil?","choices":["Four","Three","Two","One"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: hypothalamus to cord, cord to superior cervical ganglion, then to the eye. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which nerve supplies the muscle that closes the eyelids?","choices":["Third","Fourth","Fifth","Seventh"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the facial nerve supplies orbicularis oculi. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Through which structures does the afferent limb of the near response travel?","choices":["Oculomotor nerve to ciliary ganglion","Optic tract to superior colliculus","Optic nerve to lateral geniculate and visual cortex","Optic nerve to pretectal nuclei"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why the near pathway differs from the light reflex. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man asks what nystagmus actually is. Which description matches the lecture?","choices":["A fixed deviation of one eye","Loss of the pupillary light reflex","Double vision on lateral gaze","Involuntary rhythmic oscillation of the eyes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a biphasic, tremor-like oscillating movement. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which muscle constricts the pupil, and which dilates it?","choices":["Radial muscle constricts, iris sphincter dilates","Ciliary muscle constricts, iris sphincter dilates","Superior rectus constricts, inferior rectus dilates","Iris sphincter constricts, radial muscle dilates"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: miosis and mydriasis respectively. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has nystagmus that increases when she looks toward the fast phase. Which type is this?","choices":["Physiologic nystagmus","Jerk nystagmus","Latent nystagmus","Spasmus nutans"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is named for the fast beat's direction. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which receptor action explains why apraclonidine dilates a Horner pupil?","choices":["Alpha-two greater than alpha-one activity","Pure beta blockade","Anticholinesterase activity","Prostaglandin analogue activity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, acting on a denervated, supersensitive pupil. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which muscle does the trochlear nerve supply, and what are its actions?","choices":["Inferior oblique, which extorts and elevates","Lateral rectus, which abducts","Superior rectus, which elevates","Superior oblique, which intorts and depresses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What happens when a bright light is moved from the healthy eye to an eye with an afferent defect?","choices":["Neither pupil changes","Both pupils dilate","Both pupils constrict further","Only the affected pupil constricts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because the affected eye perceives the light as dimmer. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which cause does the lecture say is often behind Horner syndrome?","choices":["It is often idiopathic","It is always malignant","It is always vascular","It is usually congenital"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, before the ordered causes are considered. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 30"}]};
